  "summary": "I wrote the floating-islands scene in `src/` (`main.ts`, `sky.ts`, `islands.ts`, `clouds.ts`, `waterfall.ts`, `rng.ts`). It passes `typecheck`. I couldn't run `vite build` or open the page in a browser, so I haven't seen it render and haven't measured the frame rate. Colors, lighting and fog strength are untuned guesses.\n\n- **Sky:** a shader sphere gives an orange-to-pink-to-purple gradient with a large low sun and glow.\n- **Islands:** there are 9, with radii from 3 to 13. Each has a lumpy tapering rock underside and a grass top. Each has pine or blossom trees, glowing crystals, and small floating pebbles. Each bobs at its own speed and phase.\n- **Haze:** a lavender fog makes distant islands paler and bluer.\n- **Clouds:** 12 banks of soft cloud sprites at different depths drift sideways at different speeds and wrap around.\n- **Waterfall:** two crossed animated ribbons plus about 420 falling mist particles drop from the rim of the largest island. The fall is rotated to face the camera at the start.\n- **Camera:** it swings back and forth in an arc around the scene on a 50 s loop with no user input, and the sun stays in view throughout.\n- **Rendering contract:** it draws into the existing `#app` canvas at 640x360 and sets `window.__EVAL_READY__` after the first frame.\n- **Procedural only:** everything is built in code, with no external assets.\n\n",
